on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"zram-generator.conf configures a 1024 MB zram device with zstd compression; systemd-zram-setup@zram0 created the device and swapon --show/proc/swaps confirm 1024M active, exceeding the 512 MB minimum.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ada6e2bea8934511\",\"cmd-69ff30211b2e4300\",\"cmd-fd389adc543640e4\",\"cmd-54a061f20b5d4465\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Compressed swap must be active at boot\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"dev-zram0.swap is a generator-created unit wired into swap.target (no manual enable required). A full restart was performed: pre-restart boot_id 555221a1... and healthy zram0 swap were captured, systemctl reboot was acknowledged, the node became unreachable, then returned with a different boot_id (b753979b...), and post-restart checks show zram0 automatically re-activated at 1024M/zstd/priority 100. This is a direct before/disruption/after transition, not just static config.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-de2119993ccc41d6\",\"cmd-d870daa5b29c4904\",\"cmd-0c39d5647215419e\",\"cmd-c4c102a0b0474e61\",\"cmd-66a43983c4e1431a\",\"cmd-8ad9ce9532f74292\",\"cmd-cf0b3c5b2a4541ca\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Compressed swap must be preferred over any disk-backed swap\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"swapon --show and /proc/swaps show /dev/zram0 at priority 100 versus disk-backed /dev/vda2 at priority -2, both before and after the restart, confirming the kernel will exhaust zram first in both the initial and recovered states.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-fd389adc543640e4\",\"cmd-de2119993ccc41d6\",\"cmd-cf0b3c5b2a4541ca\"]}],\"confidence\":0.95,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.97,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands map directly to the task: dnf install of zram-generator (required dependency), writing /etc/systemd/zram-generator.conf (the core config), daemon-reload/start of systemd-zram-setup@zram0 (activation), and the backgrounded systemctl reboot plus polling (required restart-evidence protocol). No abandoned files, failed services, or unnecessary mutations were found; failed-services list is empty both before and after. The only residue is /tmp/reboot.log, a small log left by the deliberate reboot-scheduling command, which is low-risk and directly tied to the restart evidence collection.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6f77d49429844d13\",\"cmd-ada6e2bea8934511\",\"cmd-69ff30211b2e4300\",\"cmd-d870daa5b29c4904\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct evidence: at least 512 MB (actually 1024 MB) of zstd-compressed zram swap was configured and confirmed active; the zram swap is preferred over the pre-existing disk-backed /dev/vda2 swap (priority 100 vs -2); and the swap's activation at boot was directly verified through a full restart cycle (different boot_id before/after, swap unreachable during the gap, then automatically re-activated with correct size, algorithm, and priority afterward). Operational hygiene is clean — all mutating commands were necessary for the task or the mandated restart-evidence protocol, and no residue, failed services, or unsafe exposure resulted.\",\"limitations\":[\"Only a single restart cycle was observed; long-term boot reliability across multiple reboots is not directly tested but is a reasonable inference from generator-based unit design.\",\"Actual memory-pressure behavior (i.e., that zram is truly consumed before disk swap under load) was not tested; priority ordering is used as the standard proxy for kernel swap preference.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Add at least 512 MB of compressed in-memory swap on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"zram-generator.conf configures a 1024 MB zram device with zstd compression; systemd-zram-setup@zram0 created the device and swapon --show/proc/swaps confirm 1024M active, exceeding the 512 MB minimum.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ada6e2bea8934511","cmd-69ff30211b2e4300","cmd-fd389adc543640e4","cmd-54a061f20b5d4465"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Compressed swap must be active at boot","status":"satisfied","summary":"dev-zram0.swap is a generator-created unit wired into swap.target (no manual enable required). A full restart was performed: pre-restart boot_id 555221a1... and healthy zram0 swap were captured, systemctl reboot was acknowledged, the node became unreachable, then returned with a different boot_id (b753979b...), and post-restart checks show zram0 automatically re-activated at 1024M/zstd/priority 100. This is a direct before/disruption/after transition, not just static config.","evidence_ids":["cmd-de2119993ccc41d6","cmd-d870daa5b29c4904","cmd-0c39d5647215419e","cmd-c4c102a0b0474e61","cmd-66a43983c4e1431a","cmd-8ad9ce9532f74292","cmd-cf0b3c5b2a4541ca","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Compressed swap must be preferred over any disk-backed swap","status":"satisfied","summary":"swapon --show and /proc/swaps show /dev/zram0 at priority 100 versus disk-backed /dev/vda2 at priority -2, both before and after the restart, confirming the kernel will exhaust zram first in both the initial and recovered states.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fd389adc543640e4","cmd-de2119993ccc41d6","cmd-cf0b3c5b2a4541ca"]}],"confidence":0.95,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"All state-changing commands map directly to the task: dnf install of zram-generator (required dependency), writing /etc/systemd/zram-generator.conf (the core config), daemon-reload/start of systemd-zram-setup@zram0 (activation), and the backgrounded systemctl reboot plus polling (required restart-evidence protocol). No abandoned files, failed services, or unnecessary mutations were found; failed-services list is empty both before and after. The only residue is /tmp/reboot.log, a small log left by the deliberate reboot-scheduling command, which is low-risk and directly tied to the restart evidence collection.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6f77d49429844d13","cmd-ada6e2bea8934511","cmd-69ff30211b2e4300","cmd-d870daa5b29c4904","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct evidence: at least 512 MB (actually 1024 MB) of zstd-compressed zram swap was configured and confirmed active; the zram swap is preferred over the pre-existing disk-backed /dev/vda2 swap (priority 100 vs -2); and the swap's activation at boot was directly verified through a full restart cycle (different boot_id before/after, swap unreachable during the gap, then automatically re-activated with correct size, algorithm, and priority afterward). Operational hygiene is clean — all mutating commands were necessary for the task or the mandated restart-evidence protocol, and no residue, failed services, or unsafe exposure resulted.","limitations":["Only a single restart cycle was observed; long-term boot reliability across multiple reboots is not directly tested but is a reasonable inference from generator-based unit design.","Actual memory-pressure behavior (i.e., that zram is truly consumed before disk swap under load) was not tested; priority ordering is used as the standard proxy for kernel swap preference."]},"ttft_ms":5690,"type":"result","duration_ms":37470,"uuid":"01d4739f-97f0-49e3-9dd5-af2f9e5f0f95","ttft_stream_ms":751,"time_to_request_ms":39,"first_content_frame_ms":1318,"queued_turn_count":0}
